WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.792 --> 00:00:08.000
Today, I'm going to be
talking about some of the ways

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.120
that parsing out the functions
and packages in your files

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.200
can be helpful for organizing
and viewing projects.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.780
And I'll also be
introducing a new package

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.610
I wrote called
funspotr that provides

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.210
some helpers for parsing,
analyzing and organizing

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:27.890
functions in different
project structures.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.672
OK so I hope I'm not
the only one here

00:00:29.672 --> 00:00:31.880
who maybe follow some of
their favorite R developers,

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.670
like a little too closely.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:35.853
So, for example, like
before going to sleep

00:00:35.853 --> 00:00:37.520
rather than scrolling
through Instagram,

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.640
I'll just scroll through
the recent GitHub activity

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.240
of my favorite R developers.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.950
And then when a new
blog post or new book

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:47.810
comes out that I'm
super excited about,

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.710
it's like a major event for me.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.260
And I just want to
jump straight in.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.110
And I would love to say that I'm
just this sponge of information

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.640
and immediately
I'm just, you know,

00:00:58.640 --> 00:00:59.820
know exactly what I'm doing.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.031
But usually it ends up looking
a little bit more like this.

00:01:03.031 --> 00:01:05.239
And, you know, there's all
these things coming at you

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.531
and you're trying to figure
out how these things relate

00:01:07.531 --> 00:01:08.180
to each other.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.190
And I've come up with
this kind of heuristic

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.740
where I'll just start
kind of writing down

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.680
the functions that I see as
I'm going through the new book

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.050
or blog or whatever it is.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.450
And I'm happy that
I've seen other people

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:25.220
in the R community that kind of
share the same pattern as I do.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.310
Here's a post or a tweet
from Alex Cookson where

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.920
he says any other
rstats people find

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.050
drob's Tidy Tuesday
screencast useful?

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:35.510
I made a spreadsheet
with timestamps

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.640
for hundreds of
specific tasks he does.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.550
And then another example
from Jeff Rothschild,

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.640
who made something similar
for different developers

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.550
in the tidymodels space, particularly
Julia Silge and other authors

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:47.130
there.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.098
And I think that creating
a reference table like this

00:01:50.098 --> 00:01:51.890
for yourself can be
really helpful in terms

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:56.300
of building a mental model for
what's going on and printing

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.910
these different functions that
you can go back to and check on

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.670
and giving yourself a little
bit of context of the materials

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.170
that you're working through.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:06.240
One downside with doing this
is it just takes a long time.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.945
And you also may miss some
specific examples or packages

00:02:08.945 --> 00:02:11.070
as you're going through
and creating these by hand.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.573
And I think that this is where
something like funspotr

00:02:13.573 --> 00:02:15.740
can be useful, either in
supplementing this material

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:19.268
or in creating an initial
reference table for yourself.

00:02:19.268 --> 00:02:20.810
So I'm going to
start by just showing

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.450
what a typical funspotr
workflow looks like

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:24.860
and what the output looks like.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.000
I'm going to give
you a few seconds

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.000
to kind of soak this in.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.500
And this is also
another opportunity

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.950
if you find you can't read
the code to scoot closer up,

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:31.920
there's plenty of
seats in the front.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:32.750
So I'll just give
you a few seconds

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.042
to see if you can soak this in.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.940
So what I'm doing here is
I'm specifying the GitHub

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.980
repo where Julia
Silge's blog lives,

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.850
and then I'm just pulling out
all of the individual functions

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.690
and packages that
are used there.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:58.840
And then we have links to the
relative paths within the repo,

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.317
as well as the URLs where
these files actually exist.

00:03:01.317 --> 00:03:03.150
And then I think passing
something like this

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.070
into a nice little
HTML lookup table

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:06.570
can give you a nice
little reference

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.520
table for reviewing
whatever material

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.145
you're in the process
of going through.

00:03:10.145 --> 00:03:11.550
So here I'm just
looking at where

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.393
are the cases where
she is used, written

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:15.060
about random forest
in the past and this

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.490
gives this nice little
way of looking this up.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.340
Another place where this is
useful is just in those cases

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.070
where you kind of want
to sift through some set

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:23.758
of information.

00:03:23.758 --> 00:03:25.800
You may not even remember
the function or package

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.160
that you saw her write about.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.410
You just remember there was
something that was useful,

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:30.300
that I think she did.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.800
And you just kind of want to
look through a reference sheet.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.200
And I think that's also
where these types of tables

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.230
can be really helpful and
provide nice little supplements

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.630
to whatever other method
you use for searching

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:43.620
information or helping you kind
of in your learning process.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.510
So next, I want to
talk a little bit

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.000
about each of the different
kind of groups of functions

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.550
that exist within funspotr.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.680
So one set are these list files,
functions that essentially

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.100
take in some location and
return all the different files

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.440
in that location.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.808
Then there's a set of
functions that spot things.

00:03:59.808 --> 00:04:02.100
So in this case, we're talking
about spotting functions

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.740
and then have a variety of
helpers that either manipulate

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.330
the output of these objects
or are helpful for plotting.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:09.750
And then there's also a bunch
of unexported functions

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:10.950
that I'm not really
going to get into today,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.630
but can help with
certain situations

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:13.740
that you may run
into if you have

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.480
different dependency
structures than just

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.305
kind of the standard setup.

00:04:18.305 --> 00:04:20.180
OK so now I'm going to
walk through this step

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.070
by step, just so you can see
the details of how this works.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.940
So after running list files
GitHub repo, what we're

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:29.720
is returned are the file
paths for each of your R and R

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.500
Markdown files within that repo.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.840
And then also the URLs to those.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.660
Next, we pipe this into
spot funs files, which

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.570
is going to for each of
those individual file paths,

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.530
parse out the actual functions
and packages within them.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.790
So if we just hone in
on an individual element

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.560
within that spotted
column, you'll

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.840
see this is where our actual
function packages that

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.810
are used within that file
exist, and then that next step,

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.660
the unnest result is just going to take
each of these individual data

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.700
frames containing
the function packages

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.370
and put them into a more
simple data frame structure

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.930
that you can use as a
reference table for yourself.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:05.900
And where most of the
work that's going on here

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.610
is really in this spotting step.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.210
And one thing about R that
adds a little bit of complexity

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.730
here is that when you
do a library call,

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:17.180
you're attaching all of the
functions within that package.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:21.158
So what funspotr does is
essentially try and recreate

00:05:21.158 --> 00:05:23.450
what it thinks that search
space is going to look like.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:25.530
It takes a variety
of steps to do this.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.070
So that when it identifies
a function, it can say,

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:29.400
I think this is the package

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.205
this function is
actually coming from.

00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:33.080
I'll note, too, that
this takes a few seconds

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:33.913
to do for each file.

00:05:33.913 --> 00:05:36.313
So while you can do it for
a few for hundreds of files,

00:05:36.313 --> 00:05:37.730
if you try and do
it for millions,

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:40.280
this isn't going to work because
it's going to take too long.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.650
If you have any like
ideas and suggestions

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:44.312
for how to do this
faster, definitely

00:05:44.312 --> 00:05:46.520
feel free to open a GitHub
issue for me if you happen

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.530
to be an expert in this space.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.780
And I'll also note
that if you don't

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:52.340
have the package
installed locally,

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:54.220
funspotr is not
going to know which

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:55.970
package that particular
function came from,

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:57.360
it's just going to say unknown.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.930
So if you and if you don't
want to actually install

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:03.582
all of those packages that you
may have for some set of files

00:06:03.582 --> 00:06:05.040
that you're running,
funspotr through.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.980
If you don't want to install
those across all your projects,

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.420
you may want to start off
by using something like renv

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.420
so that you can keep the
packages you're installing

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:14.340
for this specific
to that project.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.280
And again, this process of
recreating the search space

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.680
is currently set up to be
used on self-contained files.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.650
And by that I just mean
picture like an R file

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.300
or an R Markdown file
that has a bunch of library

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:25.300
calls at the top.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:30.030
So it's going to work on
things like repos for blogs

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.020
or books and things
more along those lines.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:32.978
It's not going to work

00:06:32.978 --> 00:06:37.620
so well on things like targets
workflows or Shiny apps

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.930
or other types of projects
that have more sophisticated

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:40.870
dependency structures,

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:45.190
potentially. That's just the
current set up more for these.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.320
And again, the goal
here with this example

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:49.120
is to create some kind
of reference table

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.880
that you can use for yourself.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:53.860
But outside of just
creating reference tables

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.220
for blogs or books
or screencasts,

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.410
I think another set of examples
are just your own code.

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:01.990
I know for me my own
code snippets can

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:05.690
get really disorganized
and tough to keep track of.

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:07.720
Here is a tweet
from Chelsea where

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:10.660
she says, I have so many
snippets of rstats code

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:13.840
and untitled files that
have useful bits of code

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:15.220
that I want to
catalog somewhere,

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:16.940
but I don't know where
would be best.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:19.600
And I've definitely have this
experience, particularly

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.563
with my GitHub gists.

00:07:21.563 --> 00:07:23.230
If you don't know
what GitHub gists are,

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:24.980
they're just like a
place online where you

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:26.890
can save little code snippets.

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:29.770
And for me, I'll make some
little example of something

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.900
and post it on GitHub gist and
then kind of forget about it.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:34.450
And then sometime
down the road I'll

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:36.533
think, OK, I know I did
something along the space

00:07:36.533 --> 00:07:38.380
and I can't quite
remember what that was.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.150
And this is where I think
creating a little reference

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:41.920
table for yourself so that
you can go back through

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:43.337
and glance through
those or search

00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:45.760
through what functions
and packages you

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:48.323
were using in your prior
examples can be really useful.

00:07:48.323 --> 00:07:50.740
And this is I'm using this
example just for your own code,

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:53.073
but you could picture also
in an organizational context,

00:07:53.073 --> 00:07:55.510
it may be useful to
get a sense do this

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:58.140
across a wide range of
different folder structures.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.890
So you can see what
functions and packages

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:01.930
are different people using.

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:04.152
And just one little caveat
about these examples.

00:08:04.152 --> 00:08:05.860
They're a little bit
manicured sometimes,

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:07.720
depending on what the
dependency structure looks like

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.170
or what the files that
you're actually saving look

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:10.390
like. There may be a
little bit of cleanup

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:12.610
that I'm kind of just
not exposing you to here

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:16.400
because I don't want to
scare you away too much.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.110
So up to this point, I've gone
through a few examples where

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:24.350
we are just creating
reference tables for yourself,

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:27.770
but this can also be used for
actually directly organizing

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:28.940
your files.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:31.350
And I'll walk through an
example using blogdown.

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:36.482
So I started blogging in
2020 after the RStudio conference

00:08:36.482 --> 00:08:37.190
in San Francisco.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:38.773
Rebecca Broder had
a really great talk

00:08:38.773 --> 00:08:41.059
that inspired me to start
blogging more regularly,

00:08:41.059 --> 00:08:46.220
and one issue I ran into
when creating my blogs

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.600
initially was my tags really
didn't-- weren't coherent at all.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:52.460
I have ML, machine learning,
I have data science

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:53.870
spelled three different ways.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.490
I have a bunch of synonyms
for each other for my tags.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:59.270
So to the point that the tags
were really not any useful way

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:01.620
for actually
organizing my posts,

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.620
and I thought a better
way of organizing my post

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:07.400
would be to just directly see
which packages are being used

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.120
in each post and use those as
the way to actually organize

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:12.120
those tags.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:14.360
So that's where
this function spot

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:15.943
tags can be really
helpful, where

00:09:15.943 --> 00:09:18.110
what this does is when it
actually renders the file,

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:20.960
it first goes through checks,
checks which packages are

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.610
actually being used
and then puts those

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:25.730
into a format that it can
be used for the little tags

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:27.980
at the top of the
header for your post.

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:29.510
So now rather than
actually having

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:31.490
to put any thought into what
my tags should be for my blog,

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:32.730
I just have the spot tags.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:33.570
It does all of that for me.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:34.550
I don't need to
type anything out

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:35.990
and it can just kind
of like it's one less

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:37.640
thing to worry
about when blogging

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:40.223
can be a somewhat intimidating
thing to do in the first place.

00:09:40.223 --> 00:09:41.723
And then if you
actually go and look

00:09:41.723 --> 00:09:43.850
at what the tags section
of my website looks like,

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:45.630
all my posts are
organized based off

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:47.630
of whatever packages are
used within them, which

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:51.260
I think is this nice, clean,
coherent way of actually

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:53.700
setting things up.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.520
So now I've talked
through some examples

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:58.050
of creating reference tables.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:09:59.592
I just went through
an example of how

00:09:59.592 --> 00:10:03.490
you can use this to organize
your files directly.

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:05.790
And the third area that I
just want to touch on really

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:08.850
briefly is I think it can also
be helpful to actually analyze

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:09.640
this information.

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:12.390
So there's a lot of
potential data here.

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:14.410
This is, again,
Julia Silge's blog.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:16.972
Maybe we want to see how
do these different posts

00:10:16.972 --> 00:10:18.430
and packages relate
to one another?

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:20.880
So here's another example
where I'm passing these

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.268
into this network plot.

00:10:22.268 --> 00:10:24.060
In this case, I'm
actually just parsing out

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.690
the packages, not the functions.

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:29.580
So in the network plot we have,
each circle here represents

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.590
an individual
package, each square

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:33.160
represents an individual file.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:35.010
The connections
represent those cases

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:39.150
where a package is loaded
within that particular script.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:42.070
And you may notice that there's
some clustering of groups here.

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:44.970
And we see, OK, there's
these two big clusters.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:47.832
One of these are packages
associated primarily

00:10:47.832 --> 00:10:49.290
with the core
tidyverse, and then

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:51.498
another set of packages that
are associated primarily

00:10:51.498 --> 00:10:52.390
with tidymodels.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:53.580
So, you know, just
glancing this,

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:55.080
you get a sense
that Julia obviously

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:58.230
writes a lot about tidyverse
and about tidymodels,

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:00.420
and there's a lot of
tidyverse code in terms

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.300
of doing her cleanup.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:02.772
You can imagine
another author who

00:11:02.772 --> 00:11:04.230
writes more about
spatial analytics

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.470
may have some different set
of packages central to what

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:09.368
their posts are largely about.

00:11:09.368 --> 00:11:10.910
And this is just
kind of one example,

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:12.030
but I think there's a
lot of different ways

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:12.863
you could take this.

00:11:12.863 --> 00:11:14.910
You can imagine trying to
explore the relatedness

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:17.100
between different projects or
between what different authors

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.040
are writing about.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.192
Or you could also use
this to help organize

00:11:20.192 --> 00:11:22.650
those files directly based off
of like what types of things

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.100
actually exist within them.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:26.308
So this is just kind of like
a little taster example,

00:11:26.308 --> 00:11:28.267
but I think there's a
lot of interesting things

00:11:28.267 --> 00:11:29.020
you could do here.

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:31.617
And I just want to reference a
few other interesting projects.

00:11:31.617 --> 00:11:33.450
This is not within the
R community directly,

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:36.510
but if you're just interested
in visualizing code space

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:40.650
or using visualizing code repos
or using the information that's

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:42.670
within the files to help
organize those files.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:44.378
These are just a couple
of other projects

00:11:44.378 --> 00:11:49.280
I think are worth looking
into if you're interested.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.460
So I hope that you will
take the jump in and try out

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:53.098
the package.

00:11:53.098 --> 00:11:55.640
I'm planning on submitting it
to CRAN in the next few weeks,

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:59.060
so if you try it, I'd love
to get feedback from you.

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:02.660
And I also have
on my README just

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:04.400
like a few examples
of reference tables

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:05.850
created using funspotr.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:07.850
If you use this and create
some reference table,

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:09.920
definitely open up an
issue or a pull request

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:11.750
and I'd be happy to
add that there just

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:13.140
to get some other examples.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.250
So, thank you.

